# 11. Difficulties Encountered

## 11.1 Understanding the RAG Architecture

One of the initial challenges was understanding how document retrieval and LLM generation work together.

The system was broken into smaller components:

```text
Loading
→ Chunking
→ Embeddings
→ Vector Storage
→ Retrieval
→ Prompt Construction
→ LLM Generation
```

This made it easier to develop and debug each stage independently.

---

## 11.2 PDF Text Extraction

Different PDF files can have different internal structures.

PyMuPDF was used to extract text page by page while preserving page information.

This also allowed source references to be maintained throughout the pipeline.

---

## 11.3 Chunk ID Collisions

During multi-document ingestion, chunks from different documents could potentially receive identical identifiers.

For example:

```text
page_1_chunk_0
```

could occur in multiple documents.

This was resolved by including the source document name:

```text
NeerRaksha_page_1_chunk_0
sample_page_1_chunk_0
```

This ensures that chunks from different documents have unique identifiers.

---

## 11.4 Irrelevant Retrieval

A vector database can return the closest available chunks even when the user asks a question that is not covered by the documents.

For example:

```text
What is the capital of France?
```

could still return unrelated document chunks because the search must find the closest available vectors.

A retrieval distance threshold was therefore introduced.

```text
RETRIEVAL_MAX_DISTANCE=1.90
```

If no chunks meet the threshold, the system responds that the information could not be found in the provided documents.

---

## 11.5 Source Citation Handling

The system needed to preserve source information from the original PDF through:

```text
PDF
→ Page
→ Chunk
→ Vector Database
→ Retrieval
→ LLM Context
→ API Response
```

Source and page metadata were therefore stored with every chunk.

---

## 11.6 Local LLM Integration

Another challenge was connecting the Python application to a locally running LLM.

Ollama's local HTTP API was used to communicate between the Python backend and Llama 3.2 3B.

Error handling and request timeouts were added to the Ollama client.

---

## 11.7 Environment Configuration

Configuration values such as the Ollama model and retrieval threshold were moved to `.env`.

This avoids hardcoding configuration values throughout the application.

A `.env.example` file was also created to document the required configuration.

---

## 11.8 GitHub Authentication

While publishing the project, GitHub HTTPS authentication initially failed because password authentication is not supported for Git operations.

Git Credential Manager was configured and the repository was successfully pushed to GitHub.

